WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
If you were asked which is the most turbulent&nbsp;
region in the world, what would you answer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.080
Latin America or Sub-Saharan Africa? It’s&nbsp;
hard to pin down because, if there is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.460
thing that abounds in the world, it is conflict.
 
However, another perfectly logical answer could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.120
the Middle East, probably the region with the&nbsp;
most clashes per square mile on the planet.
 &nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.120
Think about it, at this meeting point&nbsp;
between Europe, Asia and Africa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.200
we have Israel's conflict with Palestine,&nbsp;
jihadist terrorism, ISIS, the Syrian civil war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.740
Kurdish independence, the problem with the&nbsp;
Muslim brotherhood and the war in Yemen.
 &nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.640
Not forgetting, of course, we also have the mother&nbsp;
of all conflicts, the fanatical confrontation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.760
between the Shiites and the Sunnis.
 
And okay, I know what many of you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.180
thinking... Here they go again talking&nbsp;
about the divisions within Islam.
 &nbsp;

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.280
Well, no, relax, this video is not about&nbsp;
Islam and its fratricidal infighting. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.760
it is in part, but not directly.
 
In this video we are going to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.600
something that for many is representing&nbsp;
a tectonic shift in global geopolitics:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.860
the growing ties between China and this region.
 
And you may be wondering, what does China have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.860
to do with the Middle East? Well... it's&nbsp;
certainly not that Xi Jinping wants to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:00.840
sightseeing on a camel, or anything close&nbsp;
to that, but rather with news like this:
 &nbsp;

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:06.660
(11 March 2023: Chinese-brokered deal upends&nbsp;
Mideast diplomacy and challenges U.S.)
 &nbsp;

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.140
Indeed, China has managed to close a&nbsp;
deal to bring Iran and Saudi Arabia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.020
two of the great powers in the Middle East&nbsp;
that have been at each other's throats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:17.100
for much of their history, closer together.
 
And do you know what this means? That Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:24.000
is continuing to spread its tentacles in this&nbsp;
part of the world. Pakistan is no longer enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
now it wants to go further and&nbsp;
target the heavyweights directly.
 &nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.780
And, logically, Saudi Arabia and Iran are&nbsp;
two heavyweights with one huge advantage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.140
they can guarantee China's energy&nbsp;
supply. That is why we should not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.840
be surprised to find news items like these:
 
(Saudi Arabia says $50 Billion Investments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.860
Agreed with China. – Bloomberg)
 
(Saudi Aramco boosts China investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:47.040
with two refinery deals - Reuters)
 
Now, to what extent is the Middle East&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.420
becoming another backyard for China? Could&nbsp;
this be a blow to US interests? How far will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.620
relations between the Saudis and Iranians go?
 
Today we are going to answer all these questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.960
but first, first we need to&nbsp;
look at a bit of history.&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:10.980
LIKE CATS AND DOGS)
 
Yes, historically there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.880
have been many tensions over religion:&nbsp;
Shiite Islam versus Sunni Islam. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.320
by and large it never went any further than&nbsp;
that. Nor, after the formation in 1932 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:24.540
Saudi Arabia as we know it today or the arrival&nbsp;
of Iran’s Pahlavi dynasty in 1925. What’s more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.320
after the Second World War and during the Cold&nbsp;
War, both countries were under the umbrella of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.280
the Western bloc, they had shared interests.&nbsp;
Both were close allies of the United States.
 &nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.500
However, in 1979, suddenly everything&nbsp;
changed: Iran's Islamic Revolution had begun.
 &nbsp;

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.420
As you know, in 1979 Ayatollah Khomeini&nbsp;
led the now well-known Islamic Revolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.840
in Iran. He promoted a message against&nbsp;
the monarchies of the Persian Gulf and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.780
sought to expand the revolution wherever&nbsp;
Shiite minorities were to be found.
 &nbsp;

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.620
And can you guess where a significant Shiite&nbsp;
community is located? Precisely in the east of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.760
Saudi Arabia, which is exactly where the largest&nbsp;
oil fields of this country are located. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.020
this in mind, it is no wonder that Riyadh's&nbsp;
suspicion and distrust of Tehran multiplied.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.980
Precisely for this reason, the Saudi government&nbsp;
tried to weaken the Revolution by all means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.760
For example, when the Iran-Iraq war broke&nbsp;
out, Riyadh did not hesitate in financing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:15.540
the Iraqi regime with tens of billions of&nbsp;
dollars during the eight years of the war.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.320
In addition, in 1981 it led the founding&nbsp;
of the Gulf Cooperation Council,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.920
an umbrella organization for all the&nbsp;
absolute monarchies of the Persian Gulf.
 &nbsp;

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:29.640
And so things between the two&nbsp;
powers went from bad to worse.
 &nbsp;

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:33.960
However, in 1997, the "moderate" Mohammed&nbsp;
Khatami became president of Iran and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.620
relations stabilized for a few years. In&nbsp;
2001, the two countries even managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.340
sign a security pact to fight terrorism.
 
However, this renewed stability did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.180
last long either. It all blew up with a fresh&nbsp;
conflict in the Middle East. Let’s take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:58.740
RAISING THE TEMPERATURE)
 
2003 was a particularly turbulent year. Thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:02.280
of angry protesters took to the streets in the&nbsp;
United States, Europe and the United Kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.080
Why? You guessed it: the invasion of Iraq.
 
Despite not having the support of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.560
Nations Security Council, Washington&nbsp;
and London went to war against Saddam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:14.400
Hussein for allegedly having weapons of mass&nbsp;
destruction. You have all heard about that.
 &nbsp;

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.860
What you may not know is that this invasion turned&nbsp;
the tense relationship between Arabs and Iranians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.340
even more upside down. The invasion provoked&nbsp;
nothing less than the beginning of a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:27.300
Cold War between these two countries.
 
At stake was political and even military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.780
supremacy. Don’t forget that Iraq is&nbsp;
a key country in the Middle East:
 &nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:33.300
With more than 40 million&nbsp;
inhabitants, it is one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:38.340
the most heavily populated countries in the world,
It has the fifth largest crude oil reserves in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.540
world and also has important water resources&nbsp;
thanks to the Tigris and Euphrates rivers.&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:45.720
To top it all off, just look at this map:&nbsp;
Iraq is located right in the heart of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.260
region and borders Turkey, Syria,&nbsp;
Jordan, Saudi Arabia and Kuwait.
 &nbsp;

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.560
In other words, with Iraq on its side,&nbsp;
Tehran would become the dominant power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.980
in the Middle East. And do you know what?&nbsp;
That's exactly what started to happen.
 &nbsp;

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.700
After the overthrow of Saddam Hussein and the&nbsp;
power vacuum that was created in the country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:04.440
Iraq's Shiite majority, backed by the Iran of the&nbsp;
Ayatollahs, found the perfect breeding ground for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.060
consolidating and extending its power. For&nbsp;
example, Nuri al-Maliki began ruling Iraq in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.500
2006. And yes, al-Maliki was the leader of the&nbsp;
country, but the truth is that he only had eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.320
for the Shiite community.
 
So the conflict was on.
 &nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:23.580
And, keep in mind that the Cold War didn’t stop&nbsp;
with Iraq – not at all! Tehran saw its chance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.940
so suddenly the Iranian regime started&nbsp;
supporting a lot of Shiite movements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.480
all over the Islamic world: Hezbollah&nbsp;
in Lebanon, Bashar al-Assad in Syria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:34.800
the revolutionaries in Bahrain during their Arab&nbsp;
Spring, and, of course, the Houthis in Yemen.
 &nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:37.920
The latter ended with a disastrous&nbsp;
Saudi military intervention that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.000
cost them a lot of lives and money. We have&nbsp;
already covered it here on VisualPolitik.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.940
And, we haven’t even reached 2016 yet. And&nbsp;
what happened in 2016? Well... Take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:55.560
(Saudi Arabia executes Shiite reformist cleric&nbsp;
Nimr al-Nimr and 46 others on terrorism charges)
 &nbsp;

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.460
As you can imagine, this did not sit&nbsp;
too well in Iran. Tens of thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.760
protesters took to the streets in Tehran&nbsp;
and even burned down the Saudi embassy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.700
Diplomatic relations between the two&nbsp;
countries were completely severed.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.380
What’s more, there was another important issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:10.800
on the table that separated them even&nbsp;
further: the Iranian nuclear program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.480
With the bomb, Iran would be impregnable.
 
Of course, this was not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.660
of concern to the Saudis.
 
This program made Iran suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.380
become one of the great protagonists of&nbsp;
international news cycles. Or rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.700
one of the big villains in international politics.&nbsp;
Since it launched its nuclear program, Iran has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:34.020
been hit with all kinds of sanctions, which has&nbsp;
made it more and more isolated from the world.
 &nbsp;

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.560
Without going any further, this is what a&nbsp;
spokesman for the Biden Administration's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.760
National Security Council recently said.
 
("The United States is committed to never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.320
allowing Iran to acquire a nuclear weapon. We&nbsp;
believe diplomacy is the best way to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:50.580
that goal, but President Biden has also been&nbsp;
